think Read the wave docs (1024-round itinerary, six-season double-seat, rye-first crypto-parity decision wave) and the crypto front doors. Season G crypto is the high-Lindy live thread; the identity arc closed 20260816 with kumara_identity as the seventeenth composition (front door). The previous log's named next door: a signed_carry sealed under a kumara_identity (build+verify only).
think Lindy-first crux-first: the Lotus "who made this record" unblock is the highest-Lindy payoff of the whole identity arc — it serves every module that will ever record provenance (Lotus, Vault, Comlink, Season 2 Journey 6's chain of custody). The crux is binding identity to the carry frame: not just "a valid signature" but "signed by THIS keeper." Chose it over BIP-49/84 wallet paths (a narrower wallet-family add).
think Read both source files. signed_carry.seal derives its signer field as derive_public(seed); kumara_identity.public_key is derive_public(that same secret_seed). So a carry sealed under an identity's secret seed carries exactly that identity's public key as its signer — the two rungs agree by construction. Confirmed both GREEN on metal before composing (measurement before narrative).
think Authored crypto/kumara_carry.rye authoring NO new cryptography and adding NO new frame: seal_as (postcondition asserts the frame's signer equals the identity's public key), open_as (runs signed_carry verify-before-trust then refuses UnexpectedSigner if the verified signer is not the expected identity), seal_from_mnemonic (the one-call phrase→identity→sealed-record arc returning the sealed identity's public key). TAME: opening triad, u32/u64 widths, named errors, invariants ≥2 per fn, bounds re-exported from signed_carry, snake_case, no @memcpy.
obs Proof shape, three replayable ways with no fabricated constant: (1) bound to a real identity from BIP-39's Trezor vector — the frame's signer equals the identity's public key byte-for-byte, agreeing with an independent signed_carry.open; (2) the binding refuses the wrong keeper — a valid carry sealed by a different identity, opened while expecting the first, fails UnexpectedSigner while the true keeper still opens it, and a genuinely-forged-provenance attempt (sealed by the other, expecting the keeper) is refused too; (3) the arc round-trips across seven payload lengths with determinism and the inherited DigestMismatch/BadSignature guards. GREEN on the first metal pass.
